## Flexible Reference Conversion

```xiom
pub interface AsRef[T] {
  fn as_ref(self: &Self) -> &T;
}
```

### Implementing AsRef

```xiom
pub type UserId = Int;

impl AsRef[Int] for UserId {
  fn as_ref(self: &UserId) -> &Int {
    return self;  // UserId is an Int, just return reference
  }
}

fn get_user(id: &Int) -> User { /* ... */ }

let uid: UserId = 42.into();
get_user(&uid);  // ERROR: expected &Int, got &UserId
get_user(uid.as_ref());  // Works: converts &UserId to &Int
```

### AsRef in Generic Code

```xiom
fn print_all[T: AsRef[Str]](items: &Vec[T]) {
  for item in items {
    io.println(item.as_ref());  // Works for Str, String, &str, etc.
  }
}

let words = Vec[Str](); words.push("hello");
print_all(&words);  // Str: AsRef[Str]

let paths = Vec[Path](); paths.push("file.txt".into());
print_all(&paths);  // Path: AsRef[Str]
```

### AsRef vs Deref

| Feature | AsRef | Deref |
|---------|-------|-------|
| Method call | Explicit .as_ref() | Automatic * deref |
| Multiple targets | Yes (multiple impls) | One (type Target) |
| Use case | Flexible APIs | Smart pointers |

### Implementing Multiple AsRef

```xiom
impl AsRef[Str] for User {
  fn as_ref(self: &User) -> &Str { return &self.name; }
}

impl AsRef[Int] for User {
  fn as_ref(self: &User) -> &Int { return &self.id; }
}
```

